WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.240
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:06.839
As you all know there’s this strange species
of human out there called vegetarians, and

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:10.140
there’s an even more peculiar species called
vegans.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:17.320
And for a long time both groups have begrudgingly
been asking the same question, “do plants

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.140
have feelings?”.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:20.920
Do they have emotions, like us?

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.679
Do they feel pain?

00:00:22.679 --> 00:00:26.939
If the answer turns out to be yes, then those
aforementioned groups of people are really

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:27.939
screwed.

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:34.420
And that may very well be the case, because
research conducted by forest scientists over

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:41.250
a 30-year-period revealed that trees have
some amazing social skills; trees can actually

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.530
talk to each other.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:48.450
And this isn’t just basic communication
either, they have an impressively large vocabulary.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.510
When you walk through the forest there are
hundreds of conversations happening right

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:59.710
below your feet but you won’t hear a word
because trees don’t communicate via speech,

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.960
but through electrical impulses, just like
the human nervous system.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.909
Underneath the soil a tree’s roots spread
out horizontally as far wide as the tree is

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:12.030
tall, if not more.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.259
As they grow sideways they also intertwine
and connect with the roots of all the other

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:18.409
trees that surround it.

00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:24.090
Not only can a tree send electrical signal
to its direct neighbours, but by relaying

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:30.159
messages trees can talk to each other that
are many trees apart.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:36.009
This interconnected network of roots is referred
to as the “woodwide web”.

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:40.960
In this sense a forest really is one singular
living organism.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.799
Even the fungi join in, connecting themselves
into the root-based social network, communicating

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:48.600
with the trees.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.770
So what could trees possibly be saying to
each other?

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.659
Well mostly “pass me some food mate”.

00:01:56.659 --> 00:02:01.560
When trees get low on certain elements, which
they use to grow such as carbon, phosphorus

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.850
and sulphur they send a request out into the
woodwibe web.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:11.670
The closest tree that has some of that elements
spare will send, the carbon for example, to

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.950
the tree that sent the request.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.900
How do they send the nutrients?

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:22.190
Through the roots of course, not only do these
magnificent biological superhighways act as

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:28.269
a communication network but they also carry
physical elements all around the forest, sharing

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:30.959
the resources out equally.

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:35.739
Trees even look out for one another, if they
notice a particular tree isn’t getting enough

00:02:35.739 --> 00:02:42.360
sunlight they slowly reconfigure their branches
to let extra light through and pass on more

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.420
vitamins and nutrients to that tree.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.280
Old and sick trees also get extra love and
care from their neighbours.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.420
They may even choose to disconnect their roots
from certain trees if they’re not welcome

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.290
in the community, such as dead, rotten trees.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:02.110
Have you ever noticed how trees seem to copy
each other, changing appearance and dropping

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:08.280
their leaves around the same time, it’s
as if they’re all perfectly synchronized,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.900
well that’s because they are; trees also
possess the ability to count.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:19.030
They collectively count the passing days and
keep track of how many warm days there are

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:22.519
throughout the summer and spring each year.

00:03:22.519 --> 00:03:27.459
They then share this knowledge throughout
the forest and use it to open their buds at

00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:29.549
the optimum time.

00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:34.379
They do the same for the cold days during
the winter so they all know exactly when to

00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:36.849
start shedding their leaves.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:45.090
Now don’t worry, as far as we know, it’s
very unlikely that trees and any other plants

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:51.750
for that matter, can feel pain, they lack
the nerves required to experience the sensation.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:57.610
But they certainly do have personalities,
there are good trees that put all their efforts

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:02.310
into helping other organisms, and bad trees
that steal more nutrients than they really

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:03.390
should be doing.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:09.040
It’s even been theorised that trees can
make decisions and have memories.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.560
They even have wars.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:19.549
Species such as beech and oak are very family-orientated
and ferociously protect their own, and yes,

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:24.330
incredibly, trees do know which trees are
their own children.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:32.360
Parent trees will pump sugar solutions through
their roots to their children to support their

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.220
growth.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:40.889
Family groups will purposely deprive other
species growing nearby to try and push them

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:42.260
out of their forest.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.650
It’s practically the ghetto out there.

